  "result": "Shell completion now handles arguments containing `#` correctly, and the full test suite passes (1914 passed, 102 skipped, 1 expected failure).\n\n**The cause:** the function that splits the command line for completion treated an unquoted `#` as the start of a comment. Everything from the `#` onward was dropped, so `cli a#b c` became `['cli', 'a']`. All four shells (bash, zsh, fish, PowerShell) use that function. The result was wrong arguments and the wrong word being completed, and in fish a crash when the current word started with `#`.\n\n**The fix:** one line in `split_arg_string` in `src/click/shell_completion.py` (`lex.commenters = \"\"`), so `#` is treated as an ordinary character. This matches how Python's standard `shlex.split()` behaves.\n\n**Tests and changelog:**\n- `tests/test_parser.py`: three new splitting cases with `#` in different positions.\n- `tests/test_shell_completion.py`: a new `test_full_complete_with_hash` test covering all four shells, plus the fish case where the word being completed is just `#`.\n- `CHANGES.md`: a new \"Version 8.5.1 \u2013 Unreleased\" entry, since 8.5.0 is already released.\n\nWith the fix temporarily removed, 8 of the new tests fail, so they do catch this bug. The fish `#` case outputs a single blank line, which is what fish completion prints when nothing matches.\n\nThis only fixes the Python side. In bash, zsh and fish, an unquoted `#` at the start of a word may still be treated as a comment by the shell itself before it reaches Click.\n\nNothing is committed yet.",
